editorial:
## Method and reasoning
Keep the best subarray ending at the current position. Start fresh whenever the previous ending sum is nonpositive. In the zero case, restarting preserves the sum and reduces the length. Compare each ending candidate with the global answer by sum, then length, then starting index. Initialize the answer below all valid sums to handle all-negative arrays.

## Walk through the sample
At position 2, extending [0] and starting at [3] both give 3. Restarting wins on length. Later singleton 3s tie on sum and length, so position 2 remains the winner.

## Complexity
Time O(n), extra space O(1).

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int n;
    cin >> n;
    long long ending = 0, best = LLONG_MIN;
    int start = 1, left = 1, right = 1;
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {
        long long x;
        cin >> x;
        if (ending <= 0) {
            ending = 0;
            start = i;
        }
        ending += x;
        int len = i - start, bestLen = right - left;
        if (ending > best ||
            (ending == best && (len < bestLen || (len == bestLen && start < left)))) {
            best = ending;
            left = start;
            right = i;
        }
    }
    cout << best << ' ' << left << ' ' << right << '\n';
}
